    "offices": {
      "President": "Elected with the vice-president for a five-year term in a modified two-round system: a candidate wins outright with more than 50% of the vote, or at least 40% and a ten-point lead; otherwise the top two meet in a run-off. Until 2009 Congress chose the president when no candidate won a majority.",
      "Chamber of Deputies": "The 130 deputies are elected for five years by a mixed system: 63 in single-member districts, 60 from closed departmental lists topped up in proportion to the presidential vote, and 7 indigenous seats chosen by customary law. It makes the laws together with the Chamber of Senators.",
      "Senate": "The 36 senators, four from each of the nine departments, are elected from closed party lists by the d'Hondt method, with seats awarded on the presidential vote. The Senate shares law-making with the Chamber of Deputies."
    }
  },
